Offscreen multisampled colour through EXT_multisampled_render_to_texture (MSRTT) where the driver has it.
bgfx used the extension only for the multisampled back buffer; an offscreen MSAA colour target (the scene, the processed
picture, the UI layers) was a multisample renderbuffer in memory plus a glBlitFramebuffer resolve into the texture: a
tile GPU wrote every sample to memory and read it back for the blit, at every framebuffer change. With MSRTT the texture
itself is attached with glFramebufferTexture2DMultisampleEXT: the samples stay in tile memory and only resolved pixels
are written.
- Colour only: a sampled, single-level 2D multisampled colour target is attached with MSRTT in every framebuffer it is
  used in. Depth/stencil keep bgfx's multisample renderbuffer (and its blit when sampled): an implicitly resolved depth
  is undefined once its framebuffer is left, and the renderer draws into the scene depth again after the post chain
  (ship foam, particles: the processed picture takes the scene depth) and the UI layers share one stencil.
- Caps-driven: the extension and its entry point, and a framebuffer of an MSRTT colour texture with a multisample
  D24S8 renderbuffer complete at 2x and 4x (the extension allows the mix when the sample counts agree; probed once at
  init). Otherwise renderbuffers and the blit as upstream. The path is logged once ("BGFX offscreen MSAA: ...").
- Discarding an MSRTT colour attachment would invalidate the resolved texture: only depth/stencil are discarded.
- The sample count is the texture's, reset by every TextureGL::init: bgfx reuses a freed handle's TextureGL, and a
  single-sample target made in a freed MSRTT target's slot was attached multisampled (incomplete next to its
  single-sample depth: black 3D after «Высокая» -> «Низкая» on a Pixel 10 Pro). g_stormMsrttActive says which path
  runs; g_stormMsrttWithoutMixedProbe is a logged override for tests/renderer_bgfx/msrtt_reuse_check.cpp only.
